# Markdown

Write clear, scannable Markdown. Prefer structure over decoration.

## Structure

- One `#` title per document (or match the project’s existing heading scheme)
- Nest headings without skipping levels (`##` then `###`, not `##` then `####`)
- Prefer short sections with one purpose each
- Use lists for sequences and inventories; prefer tables only when comparing
  columns helps
- Keep line length readable in review (wrap prose around ~80–100 when the
  repo does not forbid it)

## Clarity

- Lead with the point; put background after
- Link to paths and symbols agents can open (`path/to/file.py`), not vague
  “see above”
- Prefer fenced code blocks with a language tag
- Prefer relative links inside the repo; avoid brittle absolute local paths

## Comments & asides

- HTML comments (`<!-- -->`) only for non-obvious maintainer notes — follow
  `code-comments` spirit (no ephemeral agent trails)
- Do not leave “TODO for this session” or changelog narration in docs

## Safety

- Never put secrets, tokens, or private credentials in Markdown
- Prefer placeholders (`YOUR_API_KEY`) in examples
- When documenting commands, prefer copy-pasteable blocks that are safe by
  default (no destructive flags unless the topic is destruction)

## Anti-patterns

- Walls of unheaded prose
- Heading soup with no body under a heading
- Duplicate READMEs that drift from the source of truth
- Embedding large generated dumps that belong in a linked artifact instead
